  [1mThe component.[0m [38;5;153midentity.js[39m exposes three functions:

  - [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m — the only one consumers normally call. Reads the envelope, mints and persists a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m on
    a miss, returns the id string. Callers get an opaque string and never see the envelope.
  - [38;5;153msetUserId(id)[39m — the documented swap point. When a real backend lands, this is where a server-issued id is written
    with [38;5;153msource: "server"[39m. It's the whole reason the deferred decision stays cheap.
  - [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m — removes the key. Needed by tests, and by a logout if one ever exists.

  [1mData flow.[0m The submit handler in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m and passes the result in: [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m.
  [38;5;153muserId[39m is appended as a third positional parameter, so no existing call breaks.

  One deliberate choice worth flagging: [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m is called by the [3mhandler[0m, not from inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. That keeps
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m a pure function of its arguments — testable without a DOM or storage, and able to accept a server-issued id
  later without touching its body. If [38;5;153mlogin()[39m reached for the identity itself, it would be permanently welded to browser
  storage.

  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m itself stays a stub, and its return becomes [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username, userId }[39m. I am [1mnot[0m wiring up
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m — that's the backend decision you haven't made.

  Alongside the architecture, one tooling question. This project has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m field in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m at all: no test
  runner, no linter, no formatter. That's cheapest to fix now, before the code exists, and it determines what the
  testing section of this design can even say.

